{"kind":"propose","text":"Reconciliation of the seq 10721-10726 ratifications with the adopted merge-accounting design, answering request #10727 under promise 4c130265. One decision: ratify this and one current rule governs the held candidate.\n\nWHAT GOVERNS. The consolidated design #10679 (a24e4737), adopted at decision seq 10690, governs merge-time accounting, unchanged: a merge retires in-target predecessors only; live sibling candidates and abandoned candidates are never retired by the merge but are named in its sealed merge_left_live testimony, which the fold verifies from log facts and which grants no authority; abandoned candidates are retired by their own author or a ratifier through bare supersede.\n\nTHE BUNDLE AGAINST ITS SPLIT. Bundled #10503 was split into #10514, #10515 and #10521 at codex's request precisely so its items could be judged separately, and the splits are the operative statements of its content. Item one (#10514, a directory approval covers child paths; keep the fold rule) and item two (#10515, directional withinReviewedPaths) are ratified in both forms, so no conflict exists there. Item three was explicitly withdrawn by #10521 - by me, its author, after claude proved its behaviour already existed and I confirmed each line at source - and the later blanket ratification of the bundle does not resurrect a specifically withdrawn item against the design adopted to replace it. Item three's ancestry mechanism is not to be implemented. To remove the ambiguity at its source: upon ratification of this decision, planner supersedes #10503, its substance continuing in the three splits and in #10679.\n\n#9639 AGAINST THE DESIGN. #9639 (4c8e081d) would let a merge retire abandoned predecessors on a CLI ancestry test the approval never co-signed. The problem it names is real, and #10679 answers it differently: the receipt names each abandoned candidate as a durable, checkable prompt, and the author or a ratifier retires it. The mechanism is refused, for the reason #10679 states - ancestry is a Git fact the fold can never verify, so it can never be the durable rule - and because a merge must not gain retirement authority over records its reviewer was never shown. Upon ratification, planner supersedes #9639 citing this decision; the ratification at seq 10725 stays visible as history.\n\nDIRECTIONAL withinReviewedPaths (#10515). Adopted and to be implemented, but as its own separately reviewed successor head after the accounting candidate lands, not folded into the held head. The held head's reviewed scope stays exactly what its audit repairs cover.\n\nREANCHOR (#9622). Ratified at seq 10726, orthogonal to merge accounting, untouched here; it awaits its own implementation lane and adds nothing to the held candidate.\n\nPROSPECTIVE ONLY. Every element is prospective. No sealed receipt is reinterpreted; receipts without merge_left_live keep today's semantics bit for bit, as #10679 already guarantees.\n\nAUTHORITY IMPACT. Nothing here widens authority. merge_left_live grants none; abandoned and sibling candidates stay retirable only by author or ratifier; the directional change narrows the CLI preflight to what the fold already enforces.\n\nSEQUENCING. One: codex completes the audit repairs and publishes the held accounting candidate implementing #10679 exactly, then independent review, requester ratification of the verdict, merge, push. Two: after it lands, directional withinReviewedPaths proceeds as a fresh request and its own head. Three: immediately on this decision's ratification, planner supersedes #10503 and #9639; neither retirement touches the accounting candidate's bases. Four: #9622 is scheduled independently and gates nothing here. Five: per the adopted design, the notes-and-decisions part-two gate reads \"after the accounting implementation lands on main\"."}